# What this shared library exports, and why the list is this short.
#
# Statically linking aws-lc puts a complete BoringSSL inside this object. With
# no version script the linker exports all of it: a measurement on the first
# build that linked it found 1,700 exported symbols, of which 1,684 were the
# crypto library's internals -- 304 EVP_*, 97 EC_*, 94 BIO_*, 80 RSA_*.
#
# That is not untidiness. This process already runs a second BoringSSL:
# Conscrypt, which is what the L1 TLS path uses for its RFC 5705 exporter and
# for ALPN. Two BoringSSLs in one process, both exporting the same names
# globally, is the configuration in which a symbol resolves into the wrong one
# -- and the damage would land inside TLS, silently, a long way from its cause.
#
# So the library exports its JNI entry points and nothing else. Everything the
# C side needs from the JVM it gets by calling in, not by being called by name.
# JNI_OnLoad is here because the library defines it and the runtime linker has
# to find it. lwip_jni_bridge.c caches the JavaVM* there and every C-to-Kotlin
# callback goes through that pointer, so hiding it does not fail at link time
# or at load time -- it fails when the first packet needs a callback, as
# "get_env: JavaVM is NULL", with the tunnel simply never answering.
#
# That is not hypothetical: the first version of this file listed neither, on
# the reading that the library defined neither, and two lwIP device tests went
# red with "the stack wrote nothing back within 5s" while every build gate
# stayed green. The linker had in fact said so -- it rejected JNI_OnUnload by
# name and accepted JNI_OnLoad -- and the message was misread.
#
# JNI_OnUnload stays out because it genuinely does not exist, and the NDK links
# with --no-undefined-version, so naming it is a link error rather than a
# harmless extra line. That is the right trade in both directions: this file
# cannot describe a symbol the library lacks, and the device says so when it
# lacks one the library needs.
{
    global:
        Java_*;
        JNI_OnLoad;
    local:
        *;
};
